// ruby-rt.metajs -- RUBY'S OWN SEMANTICS, in MetaJS. Layer 2 of
// docs/runtime-next-plan.md part 3, for the sixth language after the Lua pilot,
// PHP, Swift, Java, C# and Dart.
//
// languages/ruby-to-llvm-ir.abnf emits handle IR that calls 93 js_* externs over
// the full-syntax ratchet. 30 of them are generic primitives languages/lib/runtime.c
// already provides (the C floor, layer 1). The other 63 are here.
//
// Compile with:
//     mec languages/metajs-to-llvm-ir.abnf languages/lib/ruby-rt.metajs -q -rt-lib
// which is what tests/gen-ruby-rt-ll.sh does; the result is the checked-in
// languages/lib/ruby-rt.ll that ruby-to-llvm-ir.abnf links next to runtime.ll.
// -rt-lib exports every TOP-LEVEL `function js_*` below under its own C symbol,
// so nothing else in this file is visible to the linker - the helpers are
// deliberately named rb*.
//
// THE AUTHORITATIVE SPECIFICATION IS abnf/jsrt.go (Ruby has NO dedicated Go twin:
// its semantics live in the shared file, in rubyNumBin / rubyBin / rubyStr /
// rubyInspect / rubyEq / rubySpaceship / rubyFormat / rubyMethod /
// rubyArrayMethod / rubyIsA / rubyExc / memberCall and the js_r* extern table)
// plus abnf/jsrtregex.go for the Regexp and MatchData halves. This file matches
// them function for function; where it CANNOT, the site says so in full.
//
// ----- the value model -------------------------------------------------------
//
//     null / undefined     ==  nil
//     a boolean            ==  true / false
//     a PLAIN NUMBER       ==  Integer. Exact and NEVER truncated to 32 bit.
//     {__flo: true, f}     ==  Float          (Go: jsFlo)
//     {__rat: true, n, d}  ==  Rational       (Go: jsRat, sign in n, reduced)
//     {__cpx: true, re, im} == Complex        (Go: jsCpx)
//     {__sym: true, s}     ==  Symbol         (Go: jsSym)
//     a string             ==  String
//     an array             ==  Array
//     a {__dict} object    ==  Hash: keys/vals parallel arrays, INSERTION ordered
//     {__rrange, begin, end, excl}  == a Range with an OPEN end. A range with BOTH
//                              bounds is MATERIALIZED as an array by js_rrangearr,
//                              which is what the whole lowering expects.
//     {__isclass, __name, __super, ...}   == a class / module descriptor. A BUILTIN
//                              class additionally carries __rbuiltin.
//     {__rxsrc, __rxflags}            == a Regexp
//     {__rxmd, __rxsrc, __rxflags, __rxtext, __rxcaps}  == a MatchData
//     any other object     ==  an instance; its descriptor is __class. An exception
//                              instance additionally carries `args`.
//
// THE ONE PLACE THIS DIVERGES FROM THE GO TWIN, stated rather than buried:
// jsFlo / jsRat / jsCpx / jsSym are Go value STRUCTS, so two of them with the same
// contents are `===` equal and hash to the same dict key. Here they are OBJECTS,
// which are compared by IDENTITY. SYMBOLS are therefore INTERNED (rbSym below
// hands out one object per name), which restores === and dict keying exactly for
// the one of the four that a Ruby program actually uses as a hash key and compares
// with ==. Float/Rational/Complex are not interned: a Float used as a HASH KEY, or
// compared with the floor's js_seq rather than through rbEq, would compare by
// identity here and by value in Go. The emitter never emits js_seq on a number
// (checked: its js_seq sites are `x === nil`, typeof results and class
// descriptors), and rbEq/rbDictFind below compare the tower BY VALUE, so no
// reachable path sees the difference.
//
// ----- what Ruby needed from the floor, and what it did NOT ------------------
//
//  1. NO sized-integer tag and NO boxed double. Ruby's Integer is the plain,
//     un-truncated double and its Float is the {__flo} box above - the same
//     representation abnf/jsrt.go uses - so neither tag 13 nor tag 14 is reached
//     from this file at all. The si_norm question the plan asks ("is the
//     language's unboxed representation the one si_norm produces") does not arise:
//     Ruby never builds a tag-13 cell.
//  2. NO `__raw` truthy shim. compile-core's truthy() emits `icmp ne <call>, 0` on
//     core.truthyExt, so that extern must answer a RAW INTEGER - and a shim handing
//     back a boolean HANDLE gives 2 for false, which is != 0, so every condition in
//     the program would be taken, silently. `grep -n truthyExt
//     languages/ruby-to-llvm-ir.abnf` is EMPTY, and Ruby goes further than the four
//     languages that merely keep the default: it overrides truthy() itself with a
//     single unsigned handle compare `v >= 3` (only 0/1/2 - undefined/null/false -
//     are falsy, which IS Ruby truthiness). There is no extern in the path at all.
//     The compare is sound natively because runtime.c seeds H_UNDEF/H_NULL/
//     H_FALSE/H_TRUE as 0/1/2/3, the same four handles abnf/jsrt.go uses.
//  3. NO `_raw` PARAMETERS. Every selector the ruby emitter passes goes through
//     emitStr / emitNum; the bare handle(k) arguments go to js_closure, js_arg,
//     js_rargrest, js_rblockarg and js_rrangearr - the first two are floor externs
//     and the last three take a plain COUNT, so they are declared `_raw` below.
//  4. NO coroutine primitive. `grep -c 'js_genfn\|js_yield'
//     languages/ruby-to-llvm-ir.abnf` is 0: Ruby's `yield` is a call of the block
//     the caller passed, not a generator, and no test file uses Fiber or
//     Enumerator. WALL 1 does not touch this language.
//  5. NO jsChar. Ruby has no character type; `?a` is a one-character String and the
//     emitter never emits js_char / js_char_code / js_kindex.
//
// ----- WALL 3, the scope chain: lowered in the EMITTER, not here --------------
//
// js_rdefined took a SCOPE HANDLE and walked the chain. A scope is the floor's
// private storage, so layer 2 cannot open one and this could never have been
// written here. It is a pure PROBE, and an EMITTER may call js_scope_typeof, so
// ruby-to-llvm-ir.abnf now emits it as the IR helper rb_defined. PHP recorded the
// generalisation, Swift/Java/Dart confirmed it, Ruby is the fifth lowering.
//
// ----- the regular-expression engine is IMPORTED, not copied ------------------
//
// languages/lib/regex.js is the shared backtracking engine that every INTERPRETER
// grammar includes into its tag script, and abnf/jsrtregex.go is its line-by-line
// Go port. It is written in the same MetaJS this file is, so layer 2 does not need
// a third copy: `import "./regex.js"` below compiles it into ruby-rt.ll with
// everything else. That is the first time a layer-2 library has imported a shared
// source rather than restating it, and it is worth copying - the whole Regexp /
// MatchData surface cost 1,559 lines of nothing.
//
// ----- MetaJS rules that shape the code below --------------------------------
//
// docs/abnf-dialect-gotchas.md: a local (or a reassigned parameter) keeps the TYPE
// CLASS it is first assigned, so a variable that holds a number in one branch and
// an object in another is declared `var x = anytype`; there is no ASI, so every
// `return` carries its value on the same line and a value-less early return is
// BRACED; no for..in, no splice, no split/join/lastIndexOf, indexOf ignores its
// from-index, array.length is not assignable, and there are no exponent literals.

import "./regex.js"

// The shared layer-2 runtime (docs/runtime-merge-plan.md part A). It supplies
// the externs every language implements identically, parameterised by the rtk*
// hooks below.
import "./runtime.metajs"
import "./runtime-decimal.metajs"
import "./runtime-bignum.metajs"

// ----- the rtk* hooks the shared runtime calls -------------------------------
function rtkStr(v) { return rbJStr(v) }
function rtkNum(v) { return rbToF(v) }
function rtkIsChar(v) { return false }
function rtkIsArr(v) { return rbIsArr(v) }
// js_supercall's one knob. Only the four languages whose emitter actually emits
// js_supercall define it; see the note there.
function rtkCall(f, self, argv) { return f.apply(undefined, argv) }
// js_pyset's two knobs.
function rtkIsDict(v) { return rbIsDict(v) }
// RUBY'S find, not the bare `===` scan: a big Integer and an Array are both VALUE
// keys here, so the shared js_pyset stores into the entry a Ruby program means.
function rtkDictFind(keys, k) { return rbDictFind(keys, k) }

// ===================================================== module state =========
//
// Four tables the Go twin keeps on the jsrt struct (rubyClasses, rubyGlobals,
// rubyLambdas, rubyCurExc). Every key is "$"-prefixed, which is the intern-key
// rule of docs/abnf-dialect-gotchas.md: a bare user name could collide with an
// Object.prototype member and the goja and frozen halves disagree about which
// ones exist.

var rbSymTable = {}      // "$name" -> the ONE symbol object of that name
var rbClassTable = {}    // "$Name" -> the ONE builtin class object of that name
var rbGlobals = {}       // "$name" -> the value of Ruby's $name
var rbGlobalNames = {}   // "$name" -> true once assigned (defined? needs presence)
var rbLambdas = []       // the closures a lambda literal produced (identity scan)
var rbCurExc = null      // what the innermost rescue caught, for a bare `raise`

// ===================================================== the value model ======

// DELIBERATELY NOT SHARED - measured as a LOSS after it shipped. 120ba0f made
// this `return rtIsObjNotArr(v)`, a three-deep chain (rbIsObj -> rtIsObjNotArr ->
// rtIsObj) on a predicate ~30 sites ask, and it cost ruby +11.19% against a 3.73%
// spread (6.223 G -> 6.921 G, tests/bench.sh --draws 5). python's pyIsObj is the
// same body and cost +41.26%; the full write-up and the rule it produced - size
// the CALL SITE, not the body, because a shared body costs a MetaJS call and a
// MetaJS call is a scope frame - is at pyIsObj in lib/python-rt.metajs.
//
// It is NOT rtIsObj either: that one deliberately leaves the array rejection out.
function rbIsObj(v) {
    if (v === null) { return false }
    if (v === undefined) { return false }
    if (typeof v != "object") { return false }
    return !Array.isArray(v)
}

function rbTag(v, name) {
    if (!rbIsObj(v)) { return false }
    return v[name] === true
}

function rbIsSym(v) { return rbTag(v, "__sym") }
function rbIsBig(v) { return rbTag(v, "__bint") }
function rbIsFlo(v) { return rbTag(v, "__flo") }
function rbIsRat(v) { return rbTag(v, "__rat") }
function rbIsCpx(v) { return rbTag(v, "__cpx") }
function rbIsDict(v) { return rbTag(v, "__dict") }
function rbIsRange(v) { return rbTag(v, "__rrange") }
function rbIsArr(v) { return Array.isArray(v) }
function rbIsNil(v) {
    if (v === null) { return true }
    return v === undefined
}
function rbIsFn(v) { return typeof v == "function" }

// A user object: an instance carrying a __class descriptor (never a class itself).
function rbUserObj(v) {
    if (!rbIsObj(v)) { return false }
    if (v["__isclass"] === true) { return false }
    return v["__class"] !== undefined
}

// A symbol is INTERNED, so two :names are the same handle and === / dict keying
// answer what the Go twin's comparable jsSym struct answers.
function rbSym(name) {
    var k = "$" + name
    var got = rbSymTable[k]
    if (got !== undefined) { return got }
    var s = {__sym: true, s: name}
    rbSymTable[k] = s
    return s
}

// ======================================== Integer at ARBITRARY PRECISION ====
//
// Ruby's Integer has no width at all: 2**100 is an exact 31-digit value and
// 0xffffffffffffffff is 18446744073709551615, not the double next to it. An
// Integer stays a plain number while a double counts it exactly and becomes the
// {__bint} box the moment it cannot - so nothing under 2^53 changes shape, the
// hot path pays two comparisons, and only the values that genuinely need
// arbitrary precision carry the object.
//
// THE PROMOTION TRIGGER, spelled out once here because it is written inline at
// its four sites in rbNumBin and nowhere else: `result >= 2^53 || result <=
// -2^53` is sound BOTH WAYS. Every plain operand is exact and of magnitude
// <= 2^53 (anything larger is already a big and was taken by rbABigArith before
// it), so if the TRUE result is >= 2^53 the rounded double is too - rounding to
// nearest of a value at or above 2^53 cannot land below it - and if the true
// result is < 2^53 the double is exact and the guard does not fire. It is
// INLINED rather than a predicate because a MetaJS call is a scope frame and
// this is the hottest line in the language.
//
// The three twins that MUST agree with this digit for digit are the big* family
// in languages/ruby-interpreter.abnf, rubyBig* in abnf/jsrt.go (math/big) and
// the js_bigint literal ruby-to-llvm-ir.abnf emits. ./test.sh --cross diffs two
// of them on every run and tests/probe.sh runs all four legs.
//
// Sign and magnitude, the magnitude a little endian array of base 10000 limbs -
// the same representation runtime-bignum.metajs already uses for the exact
// decimal expansion of a double, so rtBigStr renders one unchanged. A limb
// product stays below 1e8, well inside a double's exact range.
//
// What is deliberately NOT here, and is stated rather than hidden: a big meeting
// a Rational, a Float or a Complex PROMOTES THROUGH THE DOUBLE (rbToF), so
// `(2**70) + Rational(1,2)` is approximate where MRI keeps it exact. Modelling
// that needs a Rational whose parts are bigs, which is a second value model.

function rbABigNorm(limbs) {
    var out = limbs
    while (out.length > 1 && out[out.length - 1] == 0) { out.pop() }
    if (out.length == 0) { out.push(0) }
    return out
}

function rbABigNew(neg, limbs) {
    var ls = rbABigNorm(limbs)
    var n = neg
    if (ls.length == 1 && ls[0] == 0) { n = false }
    return {__bint: true, neg: n, limbs: ls}
}

function rbABigIsZero(v) {
    var ls = v["limbs"]
    return ls.length == 1 && ls[0] == 0
}

// |a| <=> |b| over two NORMALIZED limb arrays.
function rbABigCmpMag(a, b) {
    if (a.length != b.length) {
        if (a.length < b.length) { return 0 - 1 }
        return 1
    }
    var i = a.length - 1
    while (i >= 0) {
        if (a[i] != b[i]) {
            if (a[i] < b[i]) { return 0 - 1 }
            return 1
        }
        i = i - 1
    }
    return 0
}

// The signed comparison - Go's big.Int.Cmp.
function rbABigCmp(x, y) {
    if (x["neg"] != y["neg"]) {
        if (x["neg"]) { return 0 - 1 }
        return 1
    }
    var c = rbABigCmpMag(x["limbs"], y["limbs"])
    if (x["neg"]) { return -c }
    return c
}

function rbABigAddMag(a, b) {
    var out = []
    var carry = 0
    var n = a.length
    if (b.length > n) { n = b.length }
    var i = 0
    while (i < n) {
        var av = i < a.length ? a[i] : 0
        var bv = i < b.length ? b[i] : 0
        var s = av + bv + carry
        if (s >= 10000) {
            carry = 1
            s = s - 10000
        } else {
            carry = 0
        }
        out.push(s)
        i = i + 1
    }
    if (carry > 0) { out.push(carry) }
    return out
}

// |a| - |b|, and the caller guarantees |a| >= |b|.
function rbABigSubMag(a, b) {
    var out = []
    var borrow = 0
    var i = 0
    while (i < a.length) {
        var bv = i < b.length ? b[i] : 0
        var s = a[i] - bv - borrow
        if (s < 0) {
            s = s + 10000
            borrow = 1
        } else {
            borrow = 0
        }
        out.push(s)
        i = i + 1
    }
    return rbABigNorm(out)
}

// A magnitude times a single limb (0..9999), as a FRESH array: the division
// below needs the multiplicand left alone, unlike rtBigMulSmall, which is
// in-place and is what the float formatter wants.
function rbABigMulSmall(limbs, k) {
    var out = []
    var carry = 0
    var i = 0
    while (i < limbs.length) {
        var v = limbs[i] * k + carry
        carry = Math.floor(v / 10000)
        out.push(v - carry * 10000)
        i = i + 1
    }
    while (carry > 0) {
        var c2 = Math.floor(carry / 10000)
        out.push(carry - c2 * 10000)
        carry = c2
    }
    return rbABigNorm(out)
}

function rbABigNeg(x) {
    var ls = []
    var i = 0
    while (i < x["limbs"].length) {
        ls.push(x["limbs"][i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    return rbABigNew(!x["neg"], ls)
}

function rbABigAbs(x) {
    var ls = []
    var i = 0
    while (i < x["limbs"].length) {
        ls.push(x["limbs"][i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    return rbABigNew(false, ls)
}

function rbABigAdd(x, y) {
    if (x["neg"] == y["neg"]) { return rbABigNew(x["neg"], rbABigAddMag(x["limbs"], y["limbs"])) }
    var c = rbABigCmpMag(x["limbs"], y["limbs"])
    if (c == 0) { return rbABigNew(false, [0]) }
    if (c > 0) { return rbABigNew(x["neg"], rbABigSubMag(x["limbs"], y["limbs"])) }
    return rbABigNew(y["neg"], rbABigSubMag(y["limbs"], x["limbs"]))
}

function rbABigSub(x, y) { return rbABigAdd(x, rbABigNeg(y)) }

function rbABigMul(x, y) {
    var a = x["limbs"]
    var b = y["limbs"]
    var out = []
    var z = 0
    while (z < a.length + b.length) {
        out.push(0)
        z = z + 1
    }
    var i = 0
    while (i < a.length) {
        var carry = 0
        var j = 0
        while (j < b.length) {
            var cur = out[i + j] + a[i] * b[j] + carry
            carry = Math.floor(cur / 10000)
            out[i + j] = cur - carry * 10000
            j = j + 1
        }
        var k = i + b.length
        while (carry > 0) {
            var cur2 = out[k] + carry
            carry = Math.floor(cur2 / 10000)
            out[k] = cur2 - carry * 10000
            k = k + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return rbABigNew(x["neg"] != y["neg"], rbABigNorm(out))
}

// |a| divided by |b| -> {q, r}, both NORMALIZED limb arrays. Schoolbook long
// division a limb at a time; the quotient limb is found by binary search over
// 0..9999 because there is no wide-multiply primitive to estimate it with. The
// caller has already rejected a zero divisor.
function rbABigDivMag(a, b) {
    var qms = []
    var rem = [0]
    var i = a.length - 1
    while (i >= 0) {
        // rem = rem * 10000 + a[i], which in little-endian is a PREPENDED limb.
        var shifted = [a[i]]
        if (!(rem.length == 1 && rem[0] == 0)) {
            var t = 0
            while (t < rem.length) {
                shifted.push(rem[t])
                t = t + 1
            }
        }
        rem = rbABigNorm(shifted)
        var lo = 0
        var hi = 9999
        var d = 0
        while (lo <= hi) {
            var mid = Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2)
            if (rbABigCmpMag(rbABigMulSmall(b, mid), rem) <= 0) {
                d = mid
                lo = mid + 1
            } else {
                hi = mid - 1
            }
        }
        qms.push(d)
        rem = rbABigSubMag(rem, rbABigMulSmall(b, d))
        i = i - 1
    }
    // qms came out most-significant first; the representation is little-endian.
    var q = []
    var m = qms.length - 1
    while (m >= 0) {
        q.push(qms[m])
        m = m - 1
    }
    return {q: rbABigNorm(q), r: rbABigNorm(rem)}
}

// Ruby FLOORS: -7 / 2 is -4 and -7 % 3 is 2. The magnitudes give the TRUNCATED
// pair, which is corrected by one whenever the remainder is non-zero and does
// not already carry the DIVISOR's sign - the same correction rbIDiv/rbIMod make
// on doubles, and the same one bigDivMod makes in ruby-interpreter.abnf.
function rbABigDivMod(x, y) {
    var dm = rbABigDivMag(x["limbs"], y["limbs"])
    var q = rbABigNew(x["neg"] != y["neg"], dm["q"])
    var r = rbABigNew(x["neg"], dm["r"])
    if (!rbABigIsZero(r) && r["neg"] != y["neg"]) {
        return {q: rbABigSub(q, rbABigFromNum(1)), r: rbABigAdd(r, y)}
    }
    return {q: q, r: r}
}

// Go: big.Int.String().
function rbABigStr(v) {
    var out = rtBigStr(v["limbs"])
    if (v["neg"]) { return "-" + out }
    return out
}

// Go: big.Int.SetString(text, 0). The one caller is the js_bigint literal the
// emitter builds, which has already converted every radix to exact DECIMAL and
// stripped the '_' separators, so decimal digits with an optional sign is the
// whole accepted language - exactly where Go's `!ok` fails.
function rbABigFromStr(text) {
    var s = text
    var neg = false
    var i = 0
    if (s.charAt(0) == "-") {
        neg = true
        i = 1
    } else {
        if (s.charAt(0) == "+") { i = 1 }
    }
    var digits = ""
    while (i < s.length) {
        var c = s.charAt(i)
        if (c != "_") {
            if (c < "0" || c > "9") { fail("invalid Integer literal " + text) }
            digits = digits + c
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    if (digits.length == 0) { fail("invalid Integer literal " + text) }
    var limbs = []
    var end = digits.length
    while (end > 0) {
        var from = end - 4
        if (from < 0) { from = 0 }
        limbs.push(parseInt(digits.substring(from, end), 10))
        end = from
    }
    return rbABigNew(neg, limbs)
}

// An INTEGRAL, FINITE double as an exact big - Go's big.NewFloat(f).Int(nil). An
// integral double above 2^53 is a multiple of two, so halving it down to a 53 bit
// mantissa and doubling the big back up loses nothing (the argument rtExactDec
// makes for the same operation).
function rbABigFromNum(n) {
    var neg = n < 0
    var m = Math.abs(n)
    var e = 0
    while (m > 9007199254740992) {
        m = m / 2
        e = e + 1
    }
    var ls = rtBigFromNum(m)
    var k = 0
    while (k < e) {
        ls = rbABigMulSmall(ls, 2)
        k = k + 1
    }
    return rbABigNew(neg, ls)
}

// The only exit from the boxed world, and the twin of bigOut in
// ruby-interpreter.abnf and rubyBigNarrow in abnf/jsrt.go. The reconstruction can
// ROUND when the true value is past 2^53, so the candidate is checked by
// rebuilding the limbs from it - rbABigFromNum is exact, so a mismatch means the
// double is not the value.
function rbABigNarrow(x) {
    var limbs = x["limbs"]
    if (limbs.length > 4) { return x }
    var v = 0
    var i = limbs.length - 1
    while (i >= 0) {
        v = v * 10000 + limbs[i]
        i = i - 1
    }
    if (v > 9007199254740992) { return x }
    if (rbABigCmpMag(rbABigFromNum(v)["limbs"], limbs) != 0) { return x }
    if (x["neg"]) { return -v }
    return v
}

// An EXACT big for an Integer operand: a big as itself, an integral double
// inside the exactly representable range as itself, a bool as 0/1. A Float box,
// a Rational, a non-integral double and anything past 2^53 that is not already a
// big answer null, so the caller falls back to the double path rather than
// inventing precision the operand never had.
function rbABigOperand(v) {
    if (rbIsBig(v)) { return v }
    if (typeof v == "boolean") {
        if (v) { return rbABigFromNum(1) }
        return rbABigFromNum(0)
    }
    if (typeof v == "number") {
        // A plain double past 2^53 lifts EXACTLY, which is where this differs
        // from python's pyABigOperand. A Python int that large is already boxed
        // and a bare double IS a float; in Ruby a plain number is always an
        // Integer and a large one can only have come from Float#to_i or float
        // arithmetic, where MRI's answer is the exact integer value OF THAT
        // DOUBLE. So `(1e30).to_i + 1` is 1000000000000000019884624838657, as in
        // MRI. The finiteness test is not optional: rbABigFromNum halves until it
        // reaches the mantissa and an infinity never gets there.
        if (v == Math.trunc(v) && v - v == 0) { return rbABigFromNum(v) }
    }
    return null
}

// rbABigOperand for a COMPARISON, which may also take a Float: `2**64 ==
// 1.8446744073709552e19` is true in MRI and `(2**53+1) == (2**53+1).to_f` is
// false, and neither can be settled by rounding the Integer to a double. An
// arithmetic operator must NOT use this - there the Float wins the rank and the
// Integer converts, which is the tower's rule.
function rbABigCmpOf(v) {
    if (rbIsFlo(v)) {
        var f = v["f"]
        if (f - f != 0) { return null }
        if (Math.trunc(f) != f) { return null }
        return rbABigFromNum(f)
    }
    return rbABigOperand(v)
}

// An Integer built from a double: exact past 2^53, so that the ONE shape
// invariant holds - an Integer of magnitude above 2^53 is always a big and never
// a plain double. Float#to_i and the rest of the truncating family go through
// this, which is also what keeps a big and a plain number from ever being two
// spellings of one Hash key.
function rbIntFromF(x) {
    if (x - x != 0) { return x }
    if (x <= 9007199254740992 && x >= 0 - 9007199254740992) { return x }
    return rbABigNarrow(rbABigFromNum(x))
}

// Both Integer operands exactly when AT LEAST ONE of them is a big. Ruby has ONE
// Integer type and it is arbitrary precision, so a plain int operand meeting a
// big PROMOTES - which is the opposite of ECMAScript's BigInt rule.
function rbABigPair(l, r) {
    if (!rbIsBig(l) && !rbIsBig(r)) { return null }
    var x = rbABigOperand(l)
    if (x == null) { return null }
    var y = rbABigOperand(r)
    if (y == null) { return null }
    return {x: x, y: y}
}

function rbABigPow(x, n) {
    var acc = rbABigFromNum(1)
    var i = 0
    while (i < n) {
        acc = rbABigMul(acc, x)
        i = i + 1
    }
    return acc
}

// ----- the BITWISE operators, at arbitrary precision -------------------------
//
// Go: rubyBigBits, which is math/big's And/Or/Xor/Lsh/Rsh - those are defined on
// the SIGNED value, i.e. they are already Ruby's infinite two's complement.
// There is no such primitive here, so a negative operand is written out as
// ~(|x| - 1), which gives four identities per operator over AND / OR / XOR /
// ANDNOT of NON-NEGATIVE bit strings:
//
//   -a & b  == b &~ (a-1)             -a | b  == -(((a-1) &~ b) + 1)
//   -a & -b == -(((a-1) | (b-1)) + 1) -a | -b == -(((a-1) & (b-1)) + 1)
//   -a ^ b  == -(((a-1) ^ b) + 1)     -a ^ -b == (a-1) ^ (b-1)
//
// The magnitude is carried as a LITTLE-ENDIAN array of single bits: the stored
// big is base-10000 limbs, so a bit view has to be built by repeated halving
// either way, and once it is built the increments and decrements the identities
// need are two-line loops on it rather than big additions. bigBitBin in
// ruby-interpreter.abnf is this same body.

// -2^53, as a constant: the promotion trigger tests against it once per
// arithmetic step and `0 - 9007199254740992` is a subtraction at every one.
var RB_MIN53 = 0 - 9007199254740992

var RB_BIT_AND = 0
var RB_BIT_OR = 1
var RB_BIT_XOR = 2
var RB_BIT_ANDNOT = 3
var RB_SHIFT_MAX = 1048576

function rbABitsTrim(bits) {
    var n = bits.length
    while (n > 0 && bits[n - 1] == 0) { n = n - 1 }
    var out = []
    var i = 0
    while (i < n) {
        out.push(bits[i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

// The magnitude as little-endian BITS, by repeated halving of a copy.
function rbABitsOfMag(limbs) {
    var ls = []
    var i = 0
    while (i < limbs.length) {
        ls.push(limbs[i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    var bits = []
    while (ls.length > 0) {
        var rem = 0
        var j = ls.length - 1
        while (j >= 0) {
            var cur = rem * 10000 + ls[j]
            var q = Math.floor(cur / 2)
            ls[j] = q
            rem = cur - q * 2
            j = j - 1
        }
        while (ls.length > 0 && ls[ls.length - 1] == 0) { ls.pop() }
        bits.push(rem)
    }
    return rbABitsTrim(bits)
}

// bits - 1; the caller only hands this a magnitude of at least 1.
function rbABitsDec(bits) {
    var out = []
    var i = 0
    while (i < bits.length) {
        out.push(bits[i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    var k = 0
    while (k < out.length && out[k] == 0) {
        out[k] = 1
        k = k + 1
    }
    if (k < out.length) { out[k] = 0 }
    return rbABitsTrim(out)
}

function rbABitsInc(bits) {
    var out = []
    var i = 0
    while (i < bits.length) {
        out.push(bits[i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    var k = 0
    while (k < out.length && out[k] == 1) {
        out[k] = 0
        k = k + 1
    }
    if (k == out.length) { out.push(1) } else { out[k] = 1 }
    return out
}

function rbABitsOp(kind, a, b) {
    var n = a.length
    if (b.length > n) { n = b.length }
    var out = []
    var i = 0
    while (i < n) {
        var x = i < a.length ? a[i] : 0
        var y = i < b.length ? b[i] : 0
        var r = 0
        if (kind == RB_BIT_AND) {
            if (x == 1 && y == 1) { r = 1 }
        } else if (kind == RB_BIT_OR) {
            if (x == 1 || y == 1) { r = 1 }
        } else if (kind == RB_BIT_XOR) {
            if (x != y) { r = 1 }
        } else {
            if (x == 1 && y == 0) { r = 1 }
        }
        out.push(r)
        i = i + 1
    }
    return rbABitsTrim(out)
}

function rbABitsShl(bits, k) {
    if (bits.length == 0) { return [] }
    var out = []
    var i = 0
    while (i < k) {
        out.push(0)
        i = i + 1
    }
    var j = 0
    while (j < bits.length) {
        out.push(bits[j])
        j = j + 1
    }
    return out
}

function rbABitsShr(bits, k) {
    var out = []
    var i = k
    while (i < bits.length) {
        out.push(bits[i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    return rbABitsTrim(out)
}

// A bit array back to a big, by the schoolbook doubling rbABigMulSmall already is.
function rbABigFromBits(neg, bits) {
    var limbs = [0]
    var j = bits.length - 1
    while (j >= 0) {
        limbs = rbABigMulSmall(limbs, 2)
        if (bits[j] == 1) { limbs = rbABigAddMag(limbs, [1]) }
        j = j - 1
    }
    return rbABigNew(neg, limbs)
}

// A shift count as a plain number; anything absurd answers past the cap.
function rbABitsToNum(bits) {
    if (bits.length > 40) { return RB_SHIFT_MAX + 1 }
    var acc = 0
    var p = 1
    var i = 0
    while (i < bits.length) {
        if (bits[i] == 1) { acc = acc + p }
        p = p * 2
        i = i + 1
    }
    return acc
}

// Ruby's |, &, ^, << and >> over two bigs. A NEGATIVE shift count is the
// opposite shift in Ruby (`1 << -2` is 0, `4 >> -2` is 16); null is "no exact
// form here", which only an absurd shift count reaches.
function rbABigBits(op, x, y) {
    var an = x["neg"] && !rbABigIsZero(x)
    var bn = y["neg"] && !rbABigIsZero(y)
    var a = rbABitsOfMag(x["limbs"])
    var b = rbABitsOfMag(y["limbs"])
    if (op == "<<" || op == ">>") {
        var left = op == "<<"
        if (bn) { left = !left }
        var k = rbABitsToNum(b)
        if (k > RB_SHIFT_MAX) { return null }
        if (left) { return rbABigFromBits(an, rbABitsShl(a, k)) }
        if (!an) { return rbABigFromBits(false, rbABitsShr(a, k)) }
        // floor(-a / 2^k) is -(((a-1) >> k) + 1): -5 >> 1 is -3.
        return rbABigFromBits(true, rbABitsInc(rbABitsShr(rbABitsDec(a), k)))
    }
    if (op == "&") {
        if (!an && !bn) { return rbABigFromBits(false, rbABitsOp(RB_BIT_AND, a, b)) }
        if (an && !bn) { return rbABigFromBits(false, rbABitsOp(RB_BIT_ANDNOT, b, rbABitsDec(a))) }
        if (!an && bn) { return rbABigFromBits(false, rbABitsOp(RB_BIT_ANDNOT, a, rbABitsDec(b))) }
        return rbABigFromBits(true, rbABitsInc(rbABitsOp(RB_BIT_OR, rbABitsDec(a), rbABitsDec(b))))
    }
    if (op == "|") {
        if (!an && !bn) { return rbABigFromBits(false, rbABitsOp(RB_BIT_OR, a, b)) }
        if (an && !bn) { return rbABigFromBits(true, rbABitsInc(rbABitsOp(RB_BIT_ANDNOT, rbABitsDec(a), b))) }
        if (!an && bn) { return rbABigFromBits(true, rbABitsInc(rbABitsOp(RB_BIT_ANDNOT, rbABitsDec(b), a))) }
        return rbABigFromBits(true, rbABitsInc(rbABitsOp(RB_BIT_AND, rbABitsDec(a), rbABitsDec(b))))
    }
    if (op == "^") {
        if (!an && !bn) { return rbABigFromBits(false, rbABitsOp(RB_BIT_XOR, a, b)) }
        if (an && bn) { return rbABigFromBits(false, rbABitsOp(RB_BIT_XOR, rbABitsDec(a), rbABitsDec(b))) }
        if (an) { return rbABigFromBits(true, rbABitsInc(rbABitsOp(RB_BIT_XOR, rbABitsDec(a), b))) }
        return rbABigFromBits(true, rbABitsInc(rbABitsOp(RB_BIT_XOR, a, rbABitsDec(b))))
    }
    return null
}

// One arithmetic step at arbitrary precision, over two ALREADY LIFTED bigs.
// null is "no exact form", and the caller then keeps the double path it had.
function rbABigStep(op, x, y) {
    if (op == "+") { return rbABigNarrow(rbABigAdd(x, y)) }
    if (op == "-") { return rbABigNarrow(rbABigSub(x, y)) }
    if (op == "*") { return rbABigNarrow(rbABigMul(x, y)) }
    if (op == "/" || op == "%") {
        if (rbABigIsZero(y)) { fail("divided by 0") }
        var dm = rbABigDivMod(x, y)
        if (op == "/") { return rbABigNarrow(dm["q"]) }
        return rbABigNarrow(dm["r"])
    }
    if (op == "**") {
        if (y["neg"]) { return rbMkFlo(Math.pow(rbABigToF(x), rbABigToF(y))) }
        var e = rbABigNarrow(y)
        if (typeof e != "number") { return Math.pow(rbABigToF(x), rbABigToF(y)) }
        if (e > 4096) { return Math.pow(rbABigToF(x), e) }
        return rbABigNarrow(rbABigPow(x, e))
    }
    if (op == "&" || op == "|" || op == "^" || op == "<<" || op == ">>") {
        var bb = rbABigBits(op, x, y)
        if (bb == null) { return null }
        return rbABigNarrow(bb)
    }
    return null
}

// One arithmetic step with AT LEAST ONE operand already a big. null when the
// pair is not two exact Integers at all.
function rbABigArith(op, l, r) {
    var p = rbABigPair(l, r)
    if (p == null) { return null }
    return rbABigStep(op, p["x"], p["y"])
}

// The PROMOTION redo: two plain Integers whose double answer has left the exact
// range. Both operands are plain numbers, so rbABigPair cannot fire on them and
// they are lifted explicitly.
function rbABigPromote(op, x, y) {
    var a = rbABigOperand(x)
    if (a == null) { return null }
    var b = rbABigOperand(y)
    if (b == null) { return null }
    return rbABigStep(op, a, b)
}

// A big as a double, which is where this value model stops being exact. Written
// through the decimal text on purpose: there is no other exact-to-nearest route
// from an arbitrary precision integer to a double in this dialect.
function rbABigToF(v) { return parseFloat(rbABigStr(v)) }

// An Integer that is still a plain double, as text. Ruby NEVER writes one in
// exponent form, which is what `"" + x` does from 1e21 up - `(2.0 ** 70).to_i`
// printed 1.1805916207174113e+21 where MRI prints all 22 digits.
function rbIntStr(x) {
    if (x <= 9007199254740992 && x >= 0 - 9007199254740992) { return "" + x }
    if (x - x != 0) { return "" + x }
    if (Math.trunc(x) != x) { return "" + x }
    return rbABigStr(rbABigFromNum(x))
}

// Integer#to_s(base) for a big: one magnitude division per digit, which
// rbABigDivMag already is. Go asks big.Int.Text(base) for the same answer.
function rbABigBaseStr(v, base) {
    if (rbABigIsZero(v)) { return "0" }
    var mag = v["limbs"]
    var out = ""
    while (!(mag.length == 1 && mag[0] == 0)) {
        var dm = rbABigDivMag(mag, [base])
        out = RT_DIGITS.charAt(dm["r"][0]) + out
        mag = dm["q"]
    }
    if (v["neg"]) { return "-" + out }
    return out
}

// The Integer method set for a value past 2^53, and the twin of bigMethod in
// ruby-interpreter.abnf and rubyBigMethod in abnf/jsrt.go. Every step is routed
// through the exact arithmetic instead of the double - `(2**64).abs` answered
// 1.8446744073709552e+19 through rbToF. `undefined` hands the name back to
// rbNumMethod's own list, which answers it in double precision or reports it
// unknown.
function rbABigMethod(t, name, args) {
    if (name == "to_s" || name == "inspect") {
        if (args.length > 0) { return rbABigBaseStr(t, Math.trunc(rbToF(args[0]))) }
        return rbABigStr(t)
    }
    // An Integer is already floored, ceilinged, truncated and rounded, at any
    // digit argument a Ruby program can write here.
    if (name == "to_i" || name == "to_int" || name == "truncate") { return t }
    if (name == "floor" || name == "ceil" || name == "round") { return t }
    if (name == "to_f") { return rbMkFlo(rbABigToF(t)) }
    if (name == "abs" || name == "magnitude") { return rbABigNarrow(rbABigAbs(t)) }
    if (name == "zero?") { return rbABigIsZero(t) }
    if (name == "positive?") {
        if (rbABigIsZero(t)) { return false }
        return !t["neg"]
    }
    if (name == "negative?") { return t["neg"] }
    if (name == "even?") { return rbABigDivMod(t, rbABigFromNum(2))["r"]["limbs"][0] == 0 }
    if (name == "odd?") { return rbABigDivMod(t, rbABigFromNum(2))["r"]["limbs"][0] != 0 }
    if (name == "succ" || name == "next") { return rbABigNarrow(rbABigAdd(t, rbABigFromNum(1))) }
    if (name == "pred") { return rbABigNarrow(rbABigSub(t, rbABigFromNum(1))) }
    if (name == "integer?") { return true }
    if (name == "divmod") {
        var d = rbABigOperand(rbArgAt(args, 0))
        if (d != null) {
            if (rbABigIsZero(d)) { fail("divided by 0") }
            var dm = rbABigDivMod(t, d)
            return [rbABigNarrow(dm["q"]), rbABigNarrow(dm["r"])]
        }
    }
    return undefined
}

// The js_bigint extern the emitter calls for an Integer literal past 2^53.
// Go: the "js_bigint" entry of abnf/jsrt.go, which is big.Int.SetString.
function js_bigint(text) { return rbABigFromStr(rbStr(text)) }

// ===================================================== the numeric tower ====
//
// abnf/jsrt.go's rubyNumRank / rubyToF / rubyGcd / rubyMkRat / rubyReOf /
// rubyImOf / rubyAsRat, in that order. Integer < Rational < Float < Complex;
// every arithmetic step promotes both operands to the higher rank.

// A big is rank 0: it IS an Integer, not a fifth kind of number. Meeting a
// Rational, a Float or a Complex it promotes to that rank through rbToF, i.e.
// through the double it no longer fits - the boundary stated at the head of the
// arbitrary precision section above.
function rbNumRank(v) {
    if (typeof v == "number") { return 0 }
    if (rbIsRat(v)) { return 1 }
    if (rbIsFlo(v)) { return 2 }
    if (rbIsCpx(v)) { return 3 }
    if (rbIsBig(v)) { return 0 }
    return 0 - 1
}

function rbIsNum(v) { return rbNumRank(v) >= 0 }

function rbToF(v) {
    if (typeof v == "number") { return v }
    if (rbIsFlo(v)) { return v["f"] }
    if (rbIsRat(v)) { return v["n"] / v["d"] }
    if (rbIsCpx(v)) { return v["re"] }
    if (rbIsBig(v)) { return rbABigToF(v) }
    return 0
}

function rbMkFlo(x) { return {__flo: true, f: x} }
function rbMkCpx(re, im) { return {__cpx: true, re: re, im: im} }

// ----- EXACT integer arithmetic on doubles, and why every one of these exists ---
//
// The Go twin computes Ruby's Integer `/` and `%` and rubyGcd's math.Mod in
// float64 - and on arm64 the Go compiler FUSES `x - q*y` into an FMSUB, so the
// product and the subtraction are done at INFINITE precision and only the final
// result is rounded. The C floor's clang does not fuse, so the same source gives a
// different answer the moment |q*y| leaves the exactly-representable range:
// `9007199254740991 % -3` is -2 under llvm.Run and -1 without the fusion. Measured
// on the 36,000-line probe; real ruby 2.6.10 says -2, so the FUSED answer is also
// the CORRECT one and this is layer 2's defect, not the Go twin's.
//
// rbFmaSub reproduces the fused `x - q*y` exactly, with the standard Dekker
// two-product / two-sum emulation of an FMA. It is used by BOTH the Integer and
// the Float remainder, which is where the Go source has the fusable expression.
var RB_SPLIT = 134217729              // 2^27 + 1, Dekker's splitting constant
var RB_SAFE = Math.pow(2, 500)        // an operand above this would overflow the
                                      // split, so it is SCALED first (below).
                                      // Written as a pow because the MetaJS dialect
                                      // has no exponent literals.
var RB_DOWN = Math.pow(2, 0 - 256)    // the scaling step, and its inverse
var RB_UP = Math.pow(2, 256)

// The high half of a, exactly representable in 26 bits.
function rbSplitHi(a) {
    var c = RB_SPLIT * a
    return c - (c - a)
}

// x - q*y, correctly rounded as if ONE fused multiply-add had computed it.
//
// The two-product needs both operands under 2^500 or the 2^27 split overflows, and
// a Float remainder legitimately has |q| near DBL_MAX (1e308 % 2.5). Scaling by a
// POWER OF TWO is exact, so each operand is brought down first and the exact
// product (p1 + e1) is scaled back by the same amount - |p1 * scale| is about |x|,
// so the scale-back cannot overflow.
// A zero carrying x's sign. `x * 0` is the obvious spelling AND IT IS WRONG IN
// THIS DIALECT: the tag engine keeps an integral value as an integer, so
// `-4 * 0` is the integer 0 and the sign is gone - measured, `1 / (x * 0)` is
// +Infinity for x = -4, where `1 / (z / (0 - 1))` is -Infinity. Dividing a zero
// by -1 is a float division in every engine and keeps the sign. zeroSigned in
// languages/ruby-interpreter.abnf is the same body; abnf/jsrt.go spells it
// math.Copysign(0, x), which Go has and this dialect does not.
function rbZeroSign(x) {
    var z = 0
    if (x < 0) { return z / (0 - 1) }
    if (x == 0 && 1 / x < 0) { return z / (0 - 1) }
    return z
}

function rbFmaSub(x, q, y) {
    var p = q * y
    if (p !== p) { return x - p }
    if (p - p !== 0) { return x - p }
    var qq = q
    var yy = y
    var scale = 1
    while (Math.abs(qq) > RB_SAFE) {
        qq = qq * RB_DOWN
        scale = scale * RB_UP
    }
    while (Math.abs(yy) > RB_SAFE) {
        yy = yy * RB_DOWN
        scale = scale * RB_UP
    }
    var p1 = qq * yy
    var qh = rbSplitHi(qq)
    var ql = qq - qh
    var yh = rbSplitHi(yy)
    var yl = yy - yh
    var e1 = ((qh * yh - p1) + qh * yl + ql * yh) + ql * yl
    var P = p1 * scale
    var E = e1 * scale
    // TwoSum of x and -P, then the correction by the product's own error term.
    var sm = x - P
    var bb = sm - x
    var d = (x - (sm - bb)) + (-P - bb)
    return sm + (d - E)
}

function rbGcd(a, b) {
    var x = Math.abs(a)
    var y = Math.abs(b)
    while (y != 0) {
        // math.Mod is EXACT in the Go twin (it is not the fusable expression, it is
        // a scaled subtraction loop), so the naive x - floor(x/y)*y was wrong for a
        // numerator past 2^53 and reduced 90071992547409900/3 to 30023997515803300/1.
        var t = rbFmaSub(x, Math.trunc(x / y), y)
        x = y
        y = t
    }
    return x
}

// The sign lives in the numerator and the pair is reduced, so two equal Rationals
// have equal fields - which is what makes the Go twin's value equality reachable
// through rbEq here.
function rbMkRat(n, d) {
    var nn = n
    var dd = d
    if (dd < 0) {
        nn = 0 - nn
        dd = 0 - dd
    }
    var g = rbGcd(nn, dd)
    if (g > 1) {
        nn = nn / g
        dd = dd / g
    }
    return {__rat: true, n: nn, d: dd}
}

function rbReOf(v) {
    if (rbIsCpx(v)) { return v["re"] }
    return rbToF(v)
}

function rbImOf(v) {
    if (rbIsCpx(v)) { return v["im"] }
    return 0
}

function rbAsRat(v) {
    if (rbIsRat(v)) { return v }
    return rbMkRat(rbToF(v), 1)
}

// Integer division and modulo FLOOR in Ruby (-7 / 2 is -4, -7 % 3 is 2).
function rbIDiv(a, b) {
    if (b == 0) { fail("divided by 0") }
    return Math.floor(a / b)
}

function rbIMod(a, b) {
    if (b == 0) { fail("divided by 0") }
    return rbFmaSub(a, Math.floor(a / b), b)
}

// rubyNumBin: one arithmetic step over the tower.
function rbNumBin(op, l, r) {
    // A plain number is rank 0 by definition, so the typeof answers rbNumRank
    // without calling it - two more MetaJS calls per arithmetic step off the
    // path a loop of small Integer arithmetic takes.
    var rk = 0
    var rr = 0
    if (typeof l != "number") { rk = rbNumRank(l) }
    if (typeof r != "number") { rr = rbNumRank(r) }
    if (rk < 0 || rr < 0) { fail("not a number in '" + op + "'") }
    if (rr > rk) { rk = rr }
    if (rk == 3) {
        var ar = rbReOf(l)
        var ai = rbImOf(l)
        var br = rbReOf(r)
        var bi = rbImOf(r)
        if (op == "+") { return rbMkCpx(ar + br, ai + bi) }
        if (op == "-") { return rbMkCpx(ar - br, ai - bi) }
        if (op == "*") { return rbMkCpx(ar * br - ai * bi, ar * bi + ai * br) }
        if (op == "/") {
            var den = br * br + bi * bi
            return rbMkCpx((ar * br + ai * bi) / den, (ai * br - ar * bi) / den)
        }
        fail("Complex does not support '" + op + "'")
    }
    if (rk == 1) {
        var p = rbAsRat(l)
        var q = rbAsRat(r)
        if (op == "+") { return rbMkRat(p["n"] * q["d"] + q["n"] * p["d"], p["d"] * q["d"]) }
        if (op == "-") { return rbMkRat(p["n"] * q["d"] - q["n"] * p["d"], p["d"] * q["d"]) }
        if (op == "*") { return rbMkRat(p["n"] * q["n"], p["d"] * q["d"]) }
        if (op == "/") { return rbMkRat(p["n"] * q["d"], p["d"] * q["n"]) }
    }
    if (rk >= 1) {
        var x = rbToF(l)
        var y = rbToF(r)
        if (op == "+") { return rbMkFlo(x + y) }
        if (op == "-") { return rbMkFlo(x - y) }
        if (op == "*") { return rbMkFlo(x * y) }
        if (op == "/") { return rbMkFlo(x / y) }
        // Float#% - see rubyFloMod in abnf/jsrt.go. Ruby's flodivmod IS
        // `mod = fmod(x, y); if (y*mod < 0) mod += y`, so this is that body
        // verbatim; `x - floor(x/y)*y` was an approximation of it that lost
        // every digit once floor(x/y)*y stopped being exact. rbZeroSign puts
        // back the DIVIDEND's sign that fmod's zero carries and the tag engine
        // drops.
        if (op == "%") {
            var fm = x % y
            if (fm == 0) { return rbMkFlo(rbZeroSign(x)) }
            if (y * fm < 0) { return rbMkFlo(fm + y) }
            return rbMkFlo(fm)
        }
        if (op == "**") { return rbMkFlo(Math.pow(x, y)) }
        fail("Float does not support '" + op + "'")
    }
    // Two Integers. One of them past 2^53 goes straight to the exact path;
    // otherwise the double answer is computed first and only PROMOTED when it
    // leaves the exact range, so a loop of small arithmetic pays one comparison.
    // At rank 0 an operand that is not a plain number can only be a big, so the
    // gate is two `typeof`s and NO CALL. It was `rbIsBig(l) || rbIsBig(r)`, and
    // that is three MetaJS calls a side (rbIsBig -> rbTag -> rbIsObj) on the
    // hottest line in the language: tests/bench.sh --draws 5 measured the ruby
    // row at +38.79% against a 3.26% spread. Same finding, same shape, as the
    // rbIsObj row this file's head records at +11.19%.
    // ... and the SAME two typeofs then answer rbToF without calling it, which is
    // two MetaJS calls per arithmetic step the base version was already paying.
    var a = 0
    var b = 0
    if (typeof l == "number" && typeof r == "number") {
        a = l
        b = r
    } else {
        var bx = rbABigArith(op, l, r)
        if (bx != null) { return bx }
        a = rbToF(l)
        b = rbToF(r)
    }
    // rbAOver53 is INLINED at these four sites for the same reason. RB_MIN53 is
    // a module constant and not `0 - 9007199254740992`, which is a subtraction
    // per operation on the hottest line in the language.
    if (op == "+") {
        var sa = a + b
        if (sa >= 9007199254740992 || sa <= RB_MIN53) { return rbABigOr(op, a, b, sa) }
        return sa
    }
    if (op == "-") {
        var ss = a - b
        if (ss >= 9007199254740992 || ss <= RB_MIN53) { return rbABigOr(op, a, b, ss) }
        return ss
    }
    if (op == "*") {
        var sm = a * b
        if (sm >= 9007199254740992 || sm <= RB_MIN53) { return rbABigOr(op, a, b, sm) }
        return sm
    }
    if (op == "/") { return rbIDiv(a, b) }
    if (op == "%") { return rbIMod(a, b) }
    if (op == "**") {
        if (b < 0) { return rbMkFlo(Math.pow(a, b)) }
        var pw = Math.pow(a, b)
        if (pw >= 9007199254740992 || pw <= RB_MIN53) { return rbABigOr(op, a, b, pw) }
        return pw
    }
    // & | ^ << >> are INFINITE two's complement at ARBITRARY precision in Ruby:
    // `1 << 100` is a 31-digit Integer and `-1 & 0xffffffffffffffff` is
    // 18446744073709551615. int64 IS that answer for the range it holds, which is
    // what a bit-twiddling loop pays (rbBits64 is the floor's sized-integer
    // arithmetic - MetaJS's own & | ^ are ToInt32 and would wrap at 32 bits);
    // outside it the operands promote.
    if (op == "&" || op == "|" || op == "^") {
        // Inside int32 the tag engine's own operators ARE the infinite two's
        // complement answer - the sign bit stands for exactly the endless run of
        // 1s above it - and the result is an int32 too, so nothing can round.
        // That is the loop path; everything wider goes through the bignum, where
        // the RESULT is exact by construction. It cannot go through the floor's
        // sized-integer arithmetic (rbBits64, below) instead: that answers an
        // int64 read back as a DOUBLE, and `1 | 9007199254740992` is 2^53+1,
        // which the double rounds straight back down.
        if ((a | 0) === a && (b | 0) === b) {
            if (op == "&") { return a & b }
            if (op == "|") { return a | b }
            return a ^ b
        }
        return rbABigOr(op, a, b, rbBits64(op, a, b))
    }
    if (op == "<<") {
        var sl = a * Math.pow(2, b)
        if (sl < 9007199254740992 && sl > RB_MIN53 && b >= 0 && Math.trunc(a) == a) { return sl }
        return rbABigOr(op, a, b, sl)
    }
    if (op == ">>") {
        if (b >= 0 && b <= 53 && Math.trunc(a) == a && Math.abs(a) <= 9007199254740992) { return Math.floor(a / Math.pow(2, b)) }
        return rbABigOr(op, a, b, Math.floor(a / Math.pow(2, b)))
    }
    fail("Integer does not support '" + op + "'")
    return 0
}

// An exact Integer double: integral and inside the exactly representable range.
function rbAIsExact(v) { return Math.trunc(v) == v && Math.abs(v) <= 9007199254740992 }

// The promotion, with the double answer already in hand as the fallback for a
// pair that has no exact integer form (a non-integral operand, an absurd shift
// count) - which is the behaviour those pairs always had.
function rbABigOr(op, a, b, dbl) {
    var out = rbABigPromote(op, a, b)
    if (out != null) { return out }
    return dbl
}

// & | ^ over the full int64 range, which is what `float64(int64(x) op int64(y))`
// is in the Go twin. The floor's sized-integer tag (tag 13, docs part 2) does
// exactly that arithmetic, and sintNum reads the signed 64 bit result back as a
// double - so the whole of it is four calls and no 32 bit wrap. sint() applies
// si_norm, which is precisely right here: an Integer IS "a value exact in a
// double" until it leaves the tower, so a result inside +-2^53 comes back unboxed
// and one outside it stays a cell that sintNum reads.
function rbBits64(op, a, b) {
    var x = sintRaw(sintHi(Math.trunc(a)), sintLo(Math.trunc(a)), 64, false)
    var y = sintRaw(sintHi(Math.trunc(b)), sintLo(Math.trunc(b)), 64, false)
    var code = 5
    if (op == "|") { code = 6 }
    if (op == "^") { code = 7 }
    return sintNum(sintOp(code, x, y))
}

function rbNumCmp(l, r) {
    // Two Integers of which one is past 2^53 have to be compared EXACTLY:
    // through rbToF they would both round to the same double and answer 0.
    if (typeof l != "number" || typeof r != "number") {
        if (rbIsBig(l) || rbIsBig(r)) {
            var cl = rbABigCmpOf(l)
            var cr = rbABigCmpOf(r)
            if (cl != null && cr != null) { return rbABigCmp(cl, cr) }
        }
    }
    var a = rbToF(l)
    var b = rbToF(r)
    if (a < b) { return 0 - 1 }
    if (a > b) { return 1 }
    return 0
}

// rubyNeg: unary minus over the tower. Written `0 - x` NOWHERE - the signed-zero
// trap the plan records has bitten five languages, and `0 - 0.0` is `+0.0` where
// the sign has to survive. MetaJS's own unary minus compiles to js_neg, which is
// the floor's sign flip.
function rbNeg(v) {
    if (typeof v == "number") { return -v }
    if (rbTag(v, "__bint")) { return rbABigNarrow(rbABigNeg(v)) }
    if (rbIsFlo(v)) { return rbMkFlo(-v["f"]) }
    if (rbIsRat(v)) { return {__rat: true, n: -v["n"], d: v["d"]} }
    if (rbIsCpx(v)) { return rbMkCpx(-v["re"], -v["im"]) }
    if (rbUserObj(v)) { return rbMethod(v, "-@", []) }
    fail("cannot negate a " + typeof v)
    return 0
}

// ===================================================== rendering ============

// rubyFloStr: a Float keeps its decimal point even when it is whole (1000.0), which
// is what tells it apart from the Integer 1000, and goes to exponent form outside
// RUBY'S window rather than JavaScript's. numeric.c flo_to_s: exponential when
// `decpt < -3 || (decpt > DBL_DIG (15) && decpt >= digits.length)` - the high test
// has TWO parts, because MRI goes exponential only when the fixed form would have
// to pad with zeros, the mantissa always carrying a fraction
// digit and the exponent always signed and at least two digits. The full before /
// after table and the ground truth (ruby 2.6.10p210 - Float#to_s is unchanged in
// 3.x and needs no Ruby-3 syntax to probe) are at rubyFloStr in abnf/jsrt.go.

// rtSplit10 (languages/lib/runtime.metajs) turns a > 0 into {d: significant
// digits, e: the scientific exponent}; python, ruby and swift had three copies of
// it, byte-identical bar swift's comments. rtSciText and rtFixText are the two
// halves of the rendering, also shared. What is RUBY'S is the boundary between
// them and the forced ".0" on a one-digit mantissa - `dot` - which is why
// 1e+20.to_s is "1.0e+20" where Python and Swift print "1e+20".
//
// The low bound is the SAME predicate in all three, spelled two ways: ruby's
// `decpt < -3` and python's `decpt <= -4` both mean e10 <= -5.
function rtkFloDigits(a) {
    var p = rtSplit10(a)
    var decpt = p.e + 1
    if (decpt < 0 - 3 || (decpt > 15 && decpt >= p.d.length)) { return rtSciText(p.d, p.e, true) }
    return rtFixText(p.d, decpt)
}

// rtFloStr is the shared body; the three strings are the knob. Float::NAN.to_s
// is "NaN" and Float::INFINITY.to_s is "Infinity", where Python's are lowercase.
function rbFloStr(x) { return rtFloStr(x, "NaN", "Infinity", "-Infinity") }

function rbStr(v) {
    if (rbIsNil(v)) { return "" }
    if (typeof v == "string") { return v }
    if (typeof v == "number") {
        if (v <= 9007199254740992 && v >= 0 - 9007199254740992) { return "" + v }
        return rbIntStr(v)
    }
    if (rbTag(v, "__bint")) { return rbABigStr(v) }
    if (rbIsFlo(v)) { return rbFloStr(v["f"]) }
    if (rbIsRat(v)) { return ("" + v["n"]) + "/" + ("" + v["d"]) }
    if (rbIsCpx(v)) { return ("" + v["re"]) + "+" + ("" + v["im"]) + "i" }
    if (rbIsSym(v)) { return v["s"] }
    if (rbIsArr(v)) { return rbInspect(v) }
    if (rbIsDict(v)) { return rbInspect(v) }
    if (rbIsObj(v)) {
        // A CLASS object stringifies as its name (`Foo.to_s` / "#{Foo}" == "Foo").
        if (v["__isclass"] === true) {
            var nm = v["__name"]
            if (typeof nm == "string") { return nm }
        }
        var m = rbFindMethod(v, "to_s")
        if (m !== undefined) { return rbStr(m.apply(undefined, [v])) }
        // An exception instance renders as its message, like Exception#to_s.
        if (v["args"] !== undefined) {
            var msg = rbExcMessage(v)
            if (msg !== undefined) { return "" + msg }
        }
    }
    return "" + v
}

// Ruby's #inspect: nil, quoted strings, :symbols, and the bracketed collections.
function rbInspect(v) {
    if (rbIsNil(v)) { return "nil" }
    if (typeof v == "string") { return "\"" + v + "\"" }
    if (rbIsSym(v)) { return ":" + v["s"] }
    if (rbIsArr(v)) {
        var out = "["
        var i = 0
        while (i < v.length) {
            if (i > 0) { out = out + ", " }
            out = out + rbInspect(v[i])
            i = i + 1
        }
        return out + "]"
    }
    if (rbIsDict(v)) {
        var keys = v["keys"]
        var vals = v["vals"]
        var s = "{"
        var j = 0
        while (j < keys.length) {
            if (j > 0) { s = s + ", " }
            // NO SPACES around the arrow, which is MRI: `{1=>2, "a"=>[1, 2]}`.
            s = s + rbInspect(keys[j]) + "=>" + rbInspect(vals[j])
            j = j + 1
        }
        return s + "}"
    }
    return rbStr(v)
}

// ===================================================== format ===============
//
// rubyFormat: the printf directives Ruby shares with C,
// %[-][0][+][ ][width][.prec](d|i|f|s|x|X|o|b|e|%).

// ----- EXACT decimal expansion of a double ---------------------------------
//
// strconv.FormatFloat prints the EXACT value of the double and rounds HALF TO
// EVEN, which the naive `Math.floor(a * 10^prec + 0.5)` gets wrong twice over:
// it rounds half AWAY from zero (Go's %.0f of 2.5 is "2", not "3") and it loses
// every digit past 2^53 ("%f" of 1e100 has to print all 101 of them). Both were
// measured on the differential probe.
//
// A finite double is m * 2^e with m an integer below 2^53, so its exact decimal
// is a bignum: for e >= 0 it is m doubled e times, and for e < 0 it is m
// multiplied by 5 exactly -e times with the point placed -e digits from the
// right (because 2^-e == 5^-e / 10^-e). The bignum is a little-endian array of
// base-10000 limbs; multiplying by 2 or by 5 keeps every intermediate under
// 50000 + 10000, which a double holds exactly.

// The exact decimal of |x| as {i: <integer digits>, f: <fraction digits>}.
// Round the digit string `digits` (an integer written out) to `keep` digits,
// half to EVEN, and answer the kept digits - one longer when the rounding
// carried all the way (999 -> 1000), which the caller detects by the length.
// strconv.FormatFloat(v, 'f', prec, 64), exactly: the true decimal of the double,
// cut to prec fraction digits with half-to-even rounding.
// strconv.FormatFloat(v, 'e', prec, 64): d.dddde+XX, at least two exponent digits.
function rbSci(x, prec) {
    if (x !== x) { return "NaN" }
    if (x - x !== 0) {
        if (x > 0) { return "+Inf" }
        return "-Inf"
    }
    var neg = x < 0
    if (x == 0 && 1 / x < 0) { neg = true }
    var a = neg ? -x : x
    if (a == 0) {
        var z = "0"
        if (prec > 0) {
            z = z + "."
            var zi = 0
            while (zi < prec) {
                z = z + "0"
                zi = zi + 1
            }
        }
        return (neg ? "-" : "") + z + "e+00"
    }
    var dec = rtExactDec(a)
    var ip = dec["i"]
    var fp = dec["f"]
    var all = ip + fp
    // The exponent of the first significant digit, relative to the point.
    var lead = 0
    while (lead < all.length && all.charAt(lead) == "0") { lead = lead + 1 }
    var e = ip.length - 1 - lead
    var sig = all.substring(lead, all.length)
    var kept = rtRoundHalfEven(sig, prec + 1)
    if (kept.length > prec + 1) {
        // 9.99 -> 10.0: one digit fell out of the mantissa.
        e = e + 1
        kept = kept.substring(0, prec + 1)
    }
    var m = kept.substring(0, 1)
    if (prec > 0) { m = m + "." + kept.substring(1, kept.length) }
    var es = "" + (e < 0 ? 0 - e : e)
    if (es.length < 2) { es = "0" + es }
    return (neg ? "-" : "") + m + "e" + (e < 0 ? "-" : "+") + es
}

// ----- %g / %G -------------------------------------------------------------
//
// UNIMPLEMENTED in every half until now: "g" reached rbFormat's default arm, which
// emitted the letter itself and did not even consume its argument, so
// ("%g" % 1.5) was "g". The twin (rt.rubyFormat in abnf/jsrt.go) has
// strconv.FormatFloat(v, 'g', P, 64); this is that function spelled out, and the
// two were diffed digit for digit against ruby 2.6.10 over 14 values x 4
// precisions (Float#% is unchanged in 3.x, so the row is settleable there).
//
// The rule: P is the precision, defaulting to 6, and P == 0 means 1. Round to P
// SIGNIFICANT digits; if the resulting decimal exponent X is below -4 or at least
// P, write the %e shape with P-1 fraction digits, otherwise the %f shape with
// P-1-X of them. Then strip the fraction's trailing zeros, and a bare trailing
// point with them. 999999.5 at P=6 is the row that proves the exponent has to be
// read AFTER the rounding: it becomes 1000000, so X is 6 and the answer is
// "1e+06", not "999999" or "1000000".

// The trailing zeros of a fraction, and a trailing point, removed. `t` may carry
// an exponent suffix; only the mantissa is touched.
function rbGenStrip(t) {
    var at = 0 - 1
    var k = 0
    while (k < t.length) {
        if (t.charAt(k) == "e" || t.charAt(k) == "E") {
            at = k
            break
        }
        k = k + 1
    }
    var mant = at < 0 ? t : t.substring(0, at)
    var tail = at < 0 ? "" : t.substring(at, t.length)
    var dot = false
    var j = 0
    while (j < mant.length) {
        if (mant.charAt(j) == ".") { dot = true }
        j = j + 1
    }
    if (!dot) { return t }
    while (mant.charAt(mant.length - 1) == "0") { mant = mant.substring(0, mant.length - 1) }
    if (mant.charAt(mant.length - 1) == ".") { mant = mant.substring(0, mant.length - 1) }
    return mant + tail
}

// The decimal exponent of `t`, which rbSci wrote as [-]d[.ddd]e[+-]XX.
function rbSciExp(t) {
    var at = 0 - 1
    var k = 0
    while (k < t.length) {
        if (t.charAt(k) == "e") {
            at = k
            break
        }
        k = k + 1
    }
    if (at < 0) { return 0 }
    var sign = 1
    var i = at + 1
    if (t.charAt(i) == "-") {
        sign = 0 - 1
        i = i + 1
    } else if (t.charAt(i) == "+") {
        i = i + 1
    }
    var v = 0
    while (i < t.length) {
        var d = rtDigitAt(t, i)
        if (d < 0) { break }
        v = v * 10 + d
        i = i + 1
    }
    return sign * v
}

// strconv.FormatFloat(x, 'g', prec, 64), for prec >= 1.
function rbGen(x, prec) {
    var e = rbSci(x, prec - 1)
    var ex = rbSciExp(e)
    if (ex < 0 - 4 || ex >= prec) { return rbGenStrip(e) }
    return rbGenStrip(rtFixed(x, prec - 1 - ex))
}

// ----- the # (alternate) flag ----------------------------------------------
//
// UNPARSED in all three halves until now, and the two compiled ones did not even
// reach the directive letter: the flag loop stopped at "#", the width scan stopped
// there too, and "#" itself became the conversion, so ("%#g" % 1.5) was "#g" with
// the argument left unconsumed for the NEXT directive. The interpreter swallowed
// the "#" into its spec string and then ignored it, so it printed plain %g - a
// live halves divergence that --cross never saw because no test used the flag.
// MRI: it keeps %g's trailing zeros ("%#g" % 100.0 is "100.000"), it forces a
// decimal point wherever there would be none ("%#.0f" % 1.0 is "1.", "%#.1g" %
// 1234.0 is "1.e+03"), and it puts a base prefix on %x/%X/%o/%b - but not on a
// zero. Measured against ruby 2.6.10 over the whole flag/width/precision matrix.

// A decimal point at the END of the mantissa when there is none, in front of any
// exponent suffix.
function rbAltPoint(t) {
    var at = 0 - 1
    var k = 0
    while (k < t.length) {
        if (t.charAt(k) == "e" || t.charAt(k) == "E") {
            at = k
            break
        }
        k = k + 1
    }
    var mant = at < 0 ? t : t.substring(0, at)
    var tail = at < 0 ? "" : t.substring(at, t.length)
    var j = 0
    while (j < mant.length) {
        if (mant.charAt(j) == ".") { return t }
        j = j + 1
    }
    return mant + "." + tail
}

// %g's shape with the trailing zeros KEPT - rbGen without rbGenStrip.
function rbGenAlt(x, prec) {
    var e = rbSci(x, prec - 1)
    var ex = rbSciExp(e)
    if (ex < 0 - 4 || ex >= prec) { return rbAltPoint(e) }
    return rbAltPoint(rtFixed(x, prec - 1 - ex))
}

// "0x", "0X" or "0b" for a NON-ZERO value; "" otherwise ("%#x" % 0 is "0").
// The caller keeps the length so the 0 flag can pad behind it: "%#010x" % 255 is
// "0x000000ff".
//
// The test is on the VALUE and not on the digit string, which is what the first
// spelling got wrong: a precision pads the digits, and "%#.5x" % 0 is "00000" in
// MRI with no prefix at all. Octal is a different rule again and lives in
// rbIntBody - "#" on %o means "make sure there is a leading zero", so "%#.5o" % 255
// is "00377" and MRI leaves it off the ".." two's-complement form entirely.
function rbBasePrefix(conv, v) {
    if (rbIsBig(v)) {
        if (rbABigIsZero(v)) { return "" }
    } else if (v == 0) { return "" }
    if (conv == "x") { return "0x" }
    if (conv == "X") { return "0X" }
    if (conv == "b") { return "0b" }
    return ""
}

// ----- the three integer directives -----------------------------------------
//
// %d/%i/%x/%X/%o/%b ran through rtBaseStr and `"" + Math.trunc(v)`, which are
// wrong in two independent ways past 2^53: rtBaseStr converts through int64 and
// SATURATES (1e30 came out "7fffffffffffffff") and `"" + v` prints the double's
// SHORTEST form ("1e+30"). MRI converts the Float with to_i - truncation toward
// zero - and prints the resulting Integer EXACTLY, all 31 digits of
// 1e30.to_i == 1000000000000000019884624838656. Settled against ruby 2.6.10 over
// a 61-format x 38-value table; Integer formatting is unchanged in 3.x.
//
// abnf/jsrt.go's twin has math/big and asks it for Int.Text(base). There is no
// bignum multiply here, so the exact digits come out of the double's own BINARY
// expansion - a double IS m * 2^e with m an integer - regrouped in threes for
// octal and fours for hex. Base ten is the bignum that already exists,
// rtExactDec's.
function rbDigitVal(c) {
    var v = c.charCodeAt(0)
    if (v >= 48 && v <= 57) { return v - 48 }
    if (v >= 97 && v <= 122) { return v - 97 + 10 }
    return 0
}

// The exact binary digits of a non-negative INTEGRAL double. Halving a double
// above 2^53 is exact (it has no odd values left), so the mantissa comes down
// whole and the discarded factors of two come back as trailing "0"s.
function rbBinDigits(a) {
    var m = a
    var e = 0
    while (m > 9007199254740992) {
        m = m / 2
        e = e + 1
    }
    var s = ""
    var x = m
    while (x > 0) {
        var q = Math.floor(x / 2)
        if (x - q * 2 == 1) { s = "1" + s } else { s = "0" + s }
        x = q
    }
    if (s == "") { s = "0" }
    var k = 0
    while (k < e) {
        s = s + "0"
        k = k + 1
    }
    return s
}

// Binary digits regrouped `per` at a time, which is base 8 for per == 3 and base
// 16 for per == 4.
function rbGroupBits(bits, per) {
    var s = bits
    while (s.length - Math.floor(s.length / per) * per != 0) { s = "0" + s }
    var out = ""
    var i = 0
    while (i < s.length) {
        var v = 0
        var j = 0
        while (j < per) {
            v = v * 2
            if (s.charAt(i + j) == "1") { v = v + 1 }
            j = j + 1
        }
        out = out + RT_DIGITS.charAt(v)
        i = i + per
    }
    var k = 0
    while (k < out.length - 1 && out.charAt(k) == "0") { k = k + 1 }
    return out.substring(k, out.length)
}

// The bits of a big magnitude, most significant first, as a digit string.
function rbABigBinDigits(a) {
    var bb = rbABitsOfMag(a["limbs"])
    var s = ""
    var i = bb.length - 1
    while (i >= 0) {
