// ===================================================== dicts ================
//
// dictFind / dictScan / dictAppend of abnf/jsrt.go. The Go side keeps an index
// alongside the keys array as a pure ACCELERATOR (a hit is confirmed against the
// array before it is used), so a linear scan answers exactly what it answers.
// SHARED as rtDictFind - lib/runtime.metajs. This body was byte-identical in
// dart, ruby and swift, and equal to go's goDictFind; see the argument there.
// rtDictFind is `===`, and two bigs of the same value are different objects - so
// `{2**64 => :big}[2**64]` missed. The value comparison is the FALLBACK SCAN and
// nothing else: a hit costs exactly what it did, and a miss costs one type test.
// A big is only ever the key equal of another big, which is what the Go twin's
// strictEq (its *jsBigInt arm demands two of them) and the interpreter's
// core.keyId namespace both say.
// An ARRAY (and a Hash) is a VALUE key in Ruby - Array#hash and Array#eql? are
// structural - so `{[1] => 5}[[1]]` is 5 where `===` answered nil
// (docs/todo.md 2.11). That arm rides on the SAME fallback scan and for the same
// reason, and it stays in RUBY's find rather than in rtDictFind, which nine
// languages share and in which two arrays are two objects.
